# Dart Standards

Follow Effective Dart conventions.

Prefer strong typing, final, const, early returns, small functions, named parameters for complex APIs, and exhaustive state handling.

Avoid dynamic unless genuinely necessary, force unwraps unless safety is guaranteed, unnecessary nullable values, deeply nested conditionals, and overly long methods.

Use intent-revealing names such as calculateMonthlyBudget(), isLoading, hasPermission, and canSubmit.

Do not catch exceptions without meaningful handling. Avoid swallowing errors. Preserve useful error context.
